<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    ic_audio — music note glyph (attach menu, audio bubble badge)
    ------------------------------------------------------------
    Referenced by:
      - bottom_sheet_attach.xml (option_audio leading icon)
      - future: audio message bubble type badge (Phase 7)
      - future: audio player controls in an audio bubble

    Constraints this file honors:

    1. MONOCHROME TEMPLATE.
       Single fill color (#FFFFFFFF), tinted at the consumer
       level (app:tint="?attr/colorOnSurface" in
       bottom_sheet_attach.xml). Do NOT set a brand color
       here — the fill is a template.

    2. 24dp VIEWPORT.
       Matches every other chrome icon in the app. The
       attach sheet renders it at 24dp; the audio-bubble
       badge (Phase 7) will render it at 16dp in a corner
       — the vector scales losslessly.

    3. STANDARD MATERIAL "MUSIC_NOTE" GLYPH.
       This is Google's canonical Material Design
       "music_note" icon: a single note with a filled
       note-head (oval) at the bottom-left, an ascending
       vertical stem on the right, and a short flag at
       the top. It is NOT a custom design. Every music
       and audio app uses essentially this shape, and any
       deviation would read as a rendering error.

    4. SINGLE NOTE, NOT A DOUBLE NOTE.
       The "library_music" variant has two notes side by
       side and is used to signal "a collection of audio."
       For a single audio-file affordance, the single-note
       "music_note" variant is the correct choice — a
       double-note icon would suggest "playlist" or
       "music library," not "send an audio file."

       Do NOT switch to the "library_music" variant for
       this file. If a future UI ever needs a "music
       library" affordance (e.g., a menu item for browsing
       audio files), create a separate `ic_music_library.xml`
       then. Keep this one as the single-note "music_note".

    5. SOLID, NOT OUTLINED.
       The note-head is filled solid, and the stem/flag are
       solid strokes. This matches the other four icons in
       the attach sheet (all solid glyphs), which is what
       makes the option row visually cohesive.

    6. SINGLE PATH.
       The note-head, stem, and flag are drawn as a single
       closed path — the note-head's ellipse flows directly
       into the stem, which flows into the flag, producing
       one continuous silhouette. This is the standard
       Material construction. Do NOT split into three
       sub-paths (head + stem + flag) — that would produce
       visible seams at the joins, and the three shapes
       would not merge into a single recognizable "note"
       silhouette.

    7. NO androidx.AutoMirrored.
       The music note is not directional in a way that
       benefits from mirroring. Every music app on every
       platform uses the "note-head bottom-left, stem
       ascending, flag pointing right" orientation
       regardless of text direction. Mirroring under RTL
       would produce a non-standard orientation that
       users would misread. Do not add auto-mirroring.

    Rendering result at 24dp:
      A note-head with an ascending stem and a small flag
      at the top — instant recognition as "audio / music
      note."
    ============================================================
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">

    <!--
        Canonical Material Design "music_note" path.

        Breakdown:
          M 12,3                top of the stem (note head
                                is at the bottom-left, below
                                this point)
          v 10.55               stem down to just above
                                the note-head
          c -0.59,-0.34 -1.27,-0.55 -2,-0.55
                                curve into the note-head's
                                upper-right (control points
                                express the elliptical
                                top of the note-head)
          c -2.21,0 -4,1.79 -4,4
                                large curve tracing the
                                note-head's left side
                                (radius ~4 units, centered
                                around (6, 14))
          s 1.79,4 4,4          mirror curve tracing the
                                note-head's right side
          s 4,-1.79 4,-4        mirror curve completing the
                                note-head (bottom)
          V 7                   stem back up to y=7
          h 4                   short flag horizontally
                                right (4 units)
          V 3                   flag top edge
          h -6                  back to the top of the stem
          z                     close (implicit line from
                                flag top back to stem top)

        Rendered: a filled note-head (ellipse) at the
        bottom-left, an ascending stem, and a rectangular
        flag at the top-right. Standard music note.

        The 4-unit horizontal width of the flag (from
        x=12 to x=16) is what visually separates this
        glyph from a "bell" or an "antenna" — it is the
        short horizontal extension at the top-right that
        reads as "the flag of a music note."
    -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:pathData="M12,3v10.55c-0.59,-0.34 -1.27,-0.55 -2,-0.55 -2.21,0 -4,1.79 -4,4s1.79,4 4,4 4,-1.79 4,-4V7h4V3h-6z" />

</vector>
